(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-coronary-artery-disease-quiz"]={"title":"Coronary Artery Disease Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which test is the gold standard for vasospastic angina?","choices":["Provoked spasm at angiography","Exercise treadmill test","Nuclear stress test","Coronary calcium score"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The gold standard is coronary artery spasm during angiography when ergonovine or acetylcholine is delivered, then resolved with nitroglycerin given through the catheter. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which item is scored in the HEART score?","choices":["Ejection fraction","Heart rate","Age","Blood pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The HEART (History, Electrocardiogram, Age, Risk factors, Troponin) score rates history, the electrocardiogram, age, risk factors such as diabetes and smoking, and the initial troponin. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the first-line drug therapy for vasospastic angina?","choices":["Calcium channel blockers","Nonselective beta blockers","Daily aspirin","Triptans"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. First-line therapy for vasospastic angina is a calcium channel blocker such as diltiazem or amlodipine, with smoking cessation and nitrates for symptoms. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"The sinoatrial node branch arises from the right coronary artery in about what share of people?","choices":["About 10%","About 60%","About 40%","About 95%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The right coronary artery supplies the sinoatrial node in about 60% of people and the left coronary artery, through the circumflex, in about 40%, so a bradyarrhythmia cannot pin the culprit for certain. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"After infarction, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors are especially indicated for which patients?","choices":["Reduced left ventricular function","Vasospastic angina","Right ventricular infarct","Bradycardia after infarction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors reduce myocardial remodeling (scarring) and are indicated with reduced left ventricular function to prevent dilation and heart failure; most patients go home on one. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What does plain old balloon angioplasty leave out that percutaneous coronary intervention includes?","choices":["A balloon","A guidewire","Contrast dye","A stent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Plain old balloon angioplasty opens a narrowed artery without using a stent. Percutaneous coronary intervention places a drug-eluting or non-drug-eluting stent to keep the artery open. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"Which is a proposed mechanism of microvascular angina?","choices":["Complete heart block","Endothelial dysfunction","Plaque rupture","Aortic valve narrowing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Proposed mechanisms of microvascular angina include endothelial dysfunction, myocardial ischemia, insulin resistance or diabetes, abnormal autonomic control, altered cardiac sensitivity, estrogen deficiency and altered pain perception. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Why can nitroglycerin cause shock in a right ventricular infarct?","choices":["Lost preload drops output","It triggers major bleeding","It provokes coronary spasm","It blocks the atrioventricular node"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The infarcted right ventricle is preload dependent; decreasing preload reduces the Frank-Starling effect, the ventricle fails into right-sided heart failure, output falls and shock follows. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which is a modifiable coronary risk factor?","choices":["Male sex","Age over 55","Family history","Sedentary lifestyle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Modifiable risk factors are obesity, dyslipidemia, diabetes, smoking, hypertension, excessive alcohol, sedentary lifestyle and ineffective management of stress. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23"},{"q":"At its core, what is myocardial ischemia?","choices":["An oxygen supply and demand mismatch","A failure of electrical conduction","Inflammation of the pericardial sac","A primary defect of one heart valve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic heart disease is fundamentally a mismatch between the oxygen the myocardium demands and the oxygen its coronary supply delivers: the heart writes checks its vasculature cannot cash. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is a coronary calcium score used for?","choices":["Finding the culprit artery today","Measuring the ejection fraction","Long-term risk stratification","Diagnosing acute coronary syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A cardiac computed tomography calcium score (0 to 400 or more, higher meaning higher risk) stratifies future risk. It is not a test for acute coronary syndrome. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram finding persists for months after an ST-elevation infarction?","choices":["ST elevation","Reciprocal ST depression","The Q wave","Hyperacute T waves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The infarct evolves from hyperacute T waves and ST elevation (minutes to hours) to T inversion with a Q wave (hours to a day), a coronary T at a week, and a Q wave that persists for months. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 64, 65"},{"q":"Microvascular angina is more frequent in which group?","choices":["Men","Children","Smokers","Women"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina is more frequent in females and is often associated with stress or anxiety; estrogen deficiency is among its proposed mechanisms. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Why does angina typically worsen with exertion?","choices":["Exertion lowers heart rate","Exertion dissolves the plaque","Exertion blocks the coronary valve","Exertion raises oxygen demand"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exercise and emotional stress raise myocardial oxygen demand; a narrowed artery cannot raise supply to match, so the supply and demand mismatch produces pain. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 27"},{"q":"What is the 99th-percentile high-sensitivity troponin I value for men, in ng/L?","choices":["2","20","12","30"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The high-sensitivity troponin I 99th percentile is 20 ng/L for men and 12 ng/L for women; values above them are abnormal. These sex-specific numbers are ones to know. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"After initial stabilization of unstable angina, which procedure follows?","choices":["Thrombolytic therapy","Left heart catheterization","Discharge and follow-up","Repeat stress test"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Following initial stabilization, left heart catheterization introduces a guidewire to the coronary arteries, allowing coronary angiography to find the lesion and angioplasty or stenting to open it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which stress-test electrocardiogram change is usual in stable angina?","choices":["Bundle branch block","T-wave inversion","ST elevation","New Q waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stable angina is diagnosed by stress-test-induced electrocardiogram changes, usually T-wave inversion, on an exercise treadmill test, a nuclear stress test or cardiac computed tomography. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"When is a beta blocker withheld in ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["When there is hypertension","When there is tachycardia","When pain persists","When there is bradycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ST-elevation infarction treatment includes a beta blocker if there is no bradycardia, along with nitroglycerin, aspirin and a call to the catheterization team. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which term describes passing a guidewire into the heart chambers and coronary arteries?","choices":["Left heart catheterization","Coronary angiography","Coronary angioplasty","Coronary artery bypass"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Left heart catheterization is the introduction of a guidewire and access to the chambers of the heart and the coronary arteries. The terms are not interchangeable: the catheter is the access. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is troponin best used for in chest pain?","choices":["Locating the culprit artery","Replacing the electrocardiogram","Ruling infarction out","Ruling infarction in"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-sensitivity troponin identifies who can safely go home: it is a rule-out tool rather than a positive predictor of who is definitely having a heart attack. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"Subendocardial ischemia produces which change in the overlying leads?","choices":["Hyperacute T waves","ST depression","ST elevation","Pathologic Q waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Subendocardial ischemia, limited to the inner wall, gives ST depression in the leads overlying the region; transmural ischemia through the full wall thickness gives ST elevation. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What gesture is Levine's sign?","choices":["A finger pointing to one spot","A hand gripping the left arm","A clenched fist over the sternum","An open palm flat on the chest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Levine's sign is the patient holding a clenched fist over the sternum to describe the chest discomfort, a classic gesture of ischemic chest pain. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which drug can precipitate vasospastic angina?","choices":["Cocaine","Diltiazem","Amlodipine","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Drugs that may precipitate vasospastic angina are cocaine, ephedrine, marijuana, alcohol, amphetamine and triptans. Triptans are also on the list of drugs to avoid in the condition. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 89, 92"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram pattern marks a right ventricular infarct?","choices":["ST depression in V1 to V3","ST elevation in V5 and V6","ST elevation in I and aVL","ST elevation in V1 and V4R"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A right ventricular infarct shows ST elevation in V1 and the right-sided lead V4R, with reciprocal ST depression in I and aVL; the culprit is the right coronary artery. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 62, 63"},{"q":"What separates unstable angina from non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["Response to nitroglycerin","The troponin level","Pain at rest","Where the pain radiates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elevated troponin or high-sensitivity troponin is what differentiates non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction from unstable angina, in which troponin is normal. Both can occur at rest. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 47, 86"},{"q":"Which muscle type contains no troponin?","choices":["Skeletal muscle","Diaphragm muscle","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Troponins are protein molecules of cardiac and skeletal muscle; smooth muscle cells do not contain troponins. Of the three types, troponin I is specific to the heart. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"Which thrombolytic is most commonly used today?","choices":["Streptokinase","Anistreplase","Lanoteplase","Tenecteplase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tenecteplase is the most common fibrinolytic today. Others include alteplase (tissue plasminogen activator), reteplase, streptokinase, anistreplase and lanoteplase. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which components make up an atherosclerotic plaque?","choices":["Fat, smooth muscle and fibroblasts","Fibrin, platelets and red cells","Elastin, cartilage and bone","Neutrophils, pus and bacteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atherosclerosis is built of fat, smooth muscle, fibroblasts and intracellular matrix components, with macrophages turned foam cells beneath a fibrous cap. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the 99th-percentile high-sensitivity troponin I value for women, in ng/L?","choices":["30","2","12","20"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-sensitivity troponin I has sex-specific 99th percentiles: 12 ng/L for women and 20 ng/L for men. A woman above 12 ng/L of troponin I is abnormal. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"When should a patient using nitroglycerin for angina call emergency medical services?","choices":["Pain that starts with exercise","Pain unimproved 5 minutes after one dose","Pain that needs any dose at all","Only after five doses have failed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Emergency medical services are called if the chest pain is unimproved or worsening five minutes after one nitroglycerin dose, or if three consecutive doses have failed to relieve it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 40"}]};
